\#{ The starting volume.yaml, made once and never written over. Context:
   parts (folder names). Not LaTeX: everything printed must be plain. }
# The volume: its title and its parts, in order. The volume's name is its
# folder's in .papers/ (`--volume NAME` on build-chapters). `python -m coding
# build-chapters` made this file once and never changes it: edit it by hand,
# then rebuild, and main.tex follows. A part is `intro` (the introduction),
# `results` (the analysis of every language in the volume), a language's
# Glottocode (its chapter, built from its annotation), or one of the
# progress report's: `summary`, `method` and `next` (prose only),
# `contributions` (who did what, with counts from the project's records) and
# `status` (the status and limits: how far each class has been reviewed).
# The venue names the file in planars' chapters/venues/ that sets the
# layout and how examples are printed: `book` (chapters; until the
# publisher's template arrives) or `report` (an article, its parts sections).
# To co-write in Overleaf, add `overleaf: <project ID>` (the end of the
# project's address, a new empty project for each volume); python -m coding
# sync-overleaf then keeps it and this volume's folder the same.
# Optionally `authors:` (a list of names, in the order printed) and
# `author_note:` (a footnote on them, e.g. "Authors are listed
# alphabetically."), and `intro_classes: volume` for an introduction that
# covers only the classes of the volume's languages (default `all`).
# To publish it on Zenodo (python -m coding stage-zenodo): `abstract:` (a
# paragraph, the record's description), `file_name:` (what the PDF is called,
# with a date in it, e.g. constituency-typology-report-2026-11) and, once
# Zenodo has reserved them,
# `records:` with the DOIs, as {report: ..., software: ..., <glottocode>: ...}
# (printed under the title).
title: Untitled volume
venue: book
parts:
\BLOCK{for p in parts}
  - \VAR{p}
\BLOCK{endfor}
